The belt-clip "paints nothing" report cannot be resolved from the logs we have,
because the two logs that describe the same frame disagree and neither carries a
number: the count says "drew back 10" (so drew_any is true and ten targets had a
non-empty drawn_rect) while the screen shows no woven pixels at all. A boolean
"the intersection was empty" also cannot tell a near-miss from a wild miss — and
a displaced-but-nonzero intersection names its own offset, which is the single
most useful fact available here.

So print the numbers, once per process for the first three weaving frames, in a
NAMED SPACE: window_bounds, size_, the woven buffer rect, the clip bounds, the
frosted count, every submitted rect, and per target its pending_rect, drawn_rect,
the intersection with the clip bounds and that intersection's AREA. All of it is
device px, window-relative, y-down — the space inline_3d_rects_, pending_rect,
drawn_rect, window_bounds and the |output| canvas are all documented to share.
This log exists to prove or break that claim.

It discriminates the suspects that are zero on desktop and nonzero on device: a
status-bar / display-cutout inset, SurfaceView origin vs window origin, and
dst_offset (zero on win/mac, the element's window origin on Android). Any of
them appears here as a constant offset between submitted_rects and drawn, or as
an x_woven that is nonzero but displaced.

Log-only. No behaviour change from 4c005b60, deliberately: the runtime A/B now
running on the pad must not be confounded by a browser-side change, and the
belt's fix-vs-delete verdict is deferred until that A/B says whether the woven
output had any content in it at all.

